---
// THE EDUCATION PAGE. Everything conceptual about where a seed's randomness
// comes from: the four options, what your device allows, why you would bother,
// and how it goes wrong. No procedures — those are the tool page, and a
// procedure written in two places is a procedure that will disagree with
// itself the first time a vendor moves a menu.
//
// EVERGREEN BY CONSTRUCTION. Nothing here names a specific incident. The
// advisory callout renders only while an advisory is active, so when one is
// retired this page does not quietly become a period piece.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RuleRef from '../components/RuleRef.astro';
import {
  diceVerified, methods, methodCount,
  ratedDevicesForMethod, devicesWithNoDicePath,
  kit, failureModes, failureModeCount,
} from '../data/dice.js';
import { seedCosts } from '../data/dice-table.js';
import { activeAdvisories } from '../data/advisories.js';
import { numberWord } from '../data/numbers.js';

const advisory = activeAdvisories.find((a) => a.vendor === 'Coinkite') || null;

// Device support per option, joined from the same data /wallets filters on, so
// a tile can never disagree with the comparison table.
const trim = (n) => n.replace(' (BTC-only)', '').replace('Blockstream ', '').replace('Foundation ', '');
const optionTiles = methods.map((m) => ({
  ...m,
  devices: m.key === 'device' ? null : ratedDevicesForMethod(m.key).map((d) => trim(d.name)),
}));
const noPath = devicesWithNoDicePath.map((d) => trim(d.name));
const longCost = seedCosts.find((c) => c.words === 24);
---
<Base
  title="Where your seed's randomness comes from · BitcoinKeys.guide"
  description="Your seed is one very large random number. Four ways to decide where it comes from — from letting the device pick to choosing every word yourself — and what your hardware actually allows."
  verified={diceVerified}
>
  <section class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Hardware &amp; services</p>
    <h1>Where your seed's <span class="accent">randomness</span> comes from</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Your seed is one very large random number. Everything else — every address, every key, every coin you
      will ever hold — is worked out from it. Normally the device picks that number and you have no way to
      check that it picked well. You do not have to leave it at that.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="options">Your {numberWord(methodCount)} options</h2>
    <p>
      These run from most of the randomness being yours to none of it. The last is what almost everybody does,
      and it is not a wrong answer — but it is a choice, and most people make it without noticing they made it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <ul class="opt-grid">
      {optionTiles.map((m) => (
        <li class={`opt${m.houseDefault ? ' is-default' : ''}`}>
          <p class="opt-n mono">Option {m.n}{m.houseDefault && <span class="opt-badge">our default</span>}</p>
          <h3 class="opt-h">{m.label}</h3>
          <p class="opt-tag">{m.tagline}</p>
          <p class="opt-yours"><span class="opt-k">Randomness that is yours</span>{m.yours}</p>
          <p class="opt-how">{m.how}</p>
          <p class="opt-trust"><span class="opt-k">What you are trusting</span>{m.trust}</p>
          {m.devices && (
            <p class="opt-dev">
              <span class="opt-k">Devices we rate that can do this</span>
              {m.devices.length ? m.devices.join(' · ') : 'None of them.'}
            </p>
          )}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="your-device">What your device allows</h2>
    <p>
      This is the part that surprises people. <strong>Only Coinkite lets you roll dice on the device itself</strong>,
      so options 2 and 3 belong to one maker. On a Jade or a BitBox02, option 1 is the only route your randomness
      has. And on {noPath.length ? <strong>{noPath.join(', ')}</strong> : 'several devices'} there is no route at
      all — the device generates your seed and that is the end of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of that makes those devices bad. Trezor's position is a considered one: they argue you should never
      choose your own backup, because people are far worse at randomness than they believe. That is a real
      argument and the losses back it up. It is simply worth knowing before you buy, rather than after.
    </p>
    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn" href="/roll-your-own-seed">The procedures, step by step <span class="arw">→</span></a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/wallets">Filter the comparison by this</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="why">Why anyone bothers</h2>
    <p>
      Every hardware wallet generates your seed from a random-number generator you cannot inspect. When it
      works, this is fine. The problem is the word <em>when</em>: it sits under everything else you do, and you
      will not find out it failed until the coins are gone.
    </p>
    {advisory && (
      <div class="callout warn">
        <span class="label">⚠️ This is not hypothetical</span>
        <p style="margin:0">
          {advisory.lesson} <a href={advisory.href}>Read the full advisory →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    )}
    <p>
      The argument is a narrow one, and worth keeping narrow. Supplying your own randomness does not make you
      harder to phish, does not protect a badly stored backup, and does not help at all if you write the words
      down wrong. It removes exactly one risk — <strong>that the device's randomness is not random</strong> —
      and it hands you a different one, which is that you now have to do this carefully.
    </p>
    <p>
      Which is why <strong>option 3 is our default rather than option 1</strong>. Adding your own throws to the
      device's own randomness cannot make the result worse — Coinkite says so in its own documentation — and it
      costs you a few minutes and no bookkeeping. Option 1 is the honest maximum, and it is a real evening's
      work: for a 24-word seed, about {longCost.throws} throws and {longCost.flips} coin flips, each one read
      off a printed table by hand. Do that because you want the one thing it buys, not because it sounds more
      serious.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="need">What you need</h2>
    <ul class="dlist">
      {kit.map((k) => (<li><strong>{k.t}.</strong> {k.d}</li>))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 id="wrong">The {numberWord(failureModeCount)} ways this goes wrong</h2>
    <p>
      Every one of these has cost somebody their coins, and none of them involves the maths being wrong.
      <RuleRef key="never-digital" /> covers the first and most important.
    </p>
    <ul class="dlist">
      {failureModes.map((f) => (<li><strong>{f.t}.</strong> {f.d}</li>))}
    </ul>
    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn" href="/roll-your-own-seed">Take me to the procedures <span class="arw">→</span></a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .opt-grid { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 1rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr)); }
  .opt { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .opt.is-default { border-color: var(--accent); }
  .opt-n { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .07em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 .35rem; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .opt-badge { background: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-ink); border-radius: 999px;
    padding: .1rem .55rem; font-size: .68rem; letter-spacing: .04em; }
  .opt-h { margin: 0 0 .2rem; font-size: 1.08rem; line-height: 1.25; }
  .opt-tag { margin: 0 0 .7rem; color: var(--muted); font-style: italic; }
  .opt-how { margin: 0 0 .7rem; }
  .opt-k { display: block; font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: .1rem; }
  .opt-yours, .opt-trust { margin: 0 0 .7rem; }
  .opt-dev { margin: auto 0 0; padding-top: .7rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }

  .dlist { padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  .dlist li { margin-bottom: .8rem; }
  .cta-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .7rem; margin-top: 1.4rem; }
  .btn { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; background: var(--accent); color: var(--accent-ink);
    font-weight: 700; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .9rem; padding: .65rem 1rem;
    border-radius: .6rem; text-decoration: none; }
  .btn.ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--text); border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .btn.ghost:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  .arw { margin-left: .4rem; }
</style>
